<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-023-overall> a pk:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Thompson (1917) T1917-023 — calcospherites grow by radial deposition about a centre (Harting 1872 artificial CaCO3 concretions in egg albumen) — MorphoEval overall 86.0/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "Pass 2 (v2). Every v1 remediation item is closed. Thompson's own plates are now measured: the isolated Harting calcospherite of Fig. 196 is round to 0.4 % (outer-radius CV 0.0036) with a radially striated border (radial-line index +0.57, 82 striae) around an isotropic nucleus; the Fig. 195 spherites carry radial lines about their nuclei (z = 7.1 against a random-centre null); the crowded spherites of Fig. 197 form a 6.00-neighbour mosaic that is statistically indistinguishable from an equal-rate Voronoi mosaic measured by the same pipeline (KS p = 0.64) and whose low area CV (0.28 vs Poisson 0.56) shows the nuclei were hard-core spaced spherites before impingement; the Fig. 208 spherocrystal has near-constant lamella gaps (E1), while the silver-chromate Liesegang specimen follows Thompson's 'logarithmic ratio' (P = 1.070, geometric fit RMSE 2.4 px vs arithmetic 4.7 px, gaps grow with Spearman 0.91). The model side shows that isotropic deposition rounds any nucleus (A ∝ R^-0.97) whereas the anisotropic control does not, that bands require a nucleation gap, and that scanty supply (eta ≥ 1) produces the spinous forms Thompson describes on p. 424. Verdict written to result.json: SUPPORTED (7/7 tests). Residual limits: the E5 P value is not tuned to the specimen, Fig. 195/208 centres are manual landmarks, and the ooid rounds faster than uniform offset predicts. Overall 86.0/100 (faithfulness 86.1, reproducibility 86.0); rubric morphoeval 0.1.0; checkpoint commit 5490441cf; environment: python 3.12.3, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.18.1, matplotlib 3.11.2, Pillow 13.0.0, lean 4.35.0. Dimension assessments: Model fidelity to source 88, Morphological realism / specimen match 84, Parameter expressivity 86, Morphometric validation 87, Source and data traceability 86, Code-to-model traceability 86, Reproducibility package 86. Output judged: docs/media/t1917_023_on_concretions_spicules_and_sp_comparison.png: rendered output of thompson/claims/t1917-023_on-concretions-spicules-and-spicular-skeletons.py at 5490441cf, image/png, 2054366 B, 2100×1550 px, artifact_sha256 f4073dac2bba015af572262cb22db31688b872f7eacfe942f172c750ff2040a9 (sha256 f4073dac2bba015af572262cb22db31688b872f7eacfe942f172c750ff2040a9), spec_fingerprint 2236a28ef7fc242463615a8a75db9fab22386735752514693b7c41ad182492ef. Dual provenance: spec_fingerprint: 2236a28ef7fc242463615a8a75db9fab22386735752514693b7c41ad182492ef; artifact_sha256: f4073dac2bba015af572262cb22db31688b872f7eacfe942f172c750ff2040a9; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-023/carmel_ooids_thin_section_specimen.jpg=3f1e500de229723d..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-023/liesegang_rings_silver_chromate_gelatine_specimen.jpg=2f41645ff68f6000..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-023/paper_citations.bib=550251468ff0638d..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-023/scout_manifest.json=89dbc647da5d51c9..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-023/t1917_023_on_concretions_spicules_and_sp_reference.json=23dafa48d0e5c552..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-023/thompson_1917_p421_figs195-196_harting_calcospherites.jpg=f05b4d1f9ce006b8..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-023/thompson_1917_p422_fig197_aggregated_calcospherites.jpg=65eabed4733a09dd..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-023/thompson_1917_p432_fig208-209_spherocrystal_otoliths.jpg=cbfffad55d3f82b7...}. Supersedes audit: 2026-10-28-t1917-023-on-concretions-spicules-and-sp-audit. Claim boundary: reported_in_source: Thompson (1917) pp. 421-422: Harting's CaCO3 precipitated in albumen forms 'large rounded concretions, with a more or less distinct central nucleus, and with a surrounding structure at once radiate and concentric' | p. 422: crowded calcospherites 'assume polyhedral, or often regularly hexagonal, outlines'; p. 426: pairs look like 'contiguous soap-bubbles' | p. 424: with scanty albumin / scanty supply the globules become rough 'by an outgrowth of spinous or digitiform projections' | p. 427: Liesegang zones 'stand farther and farther apart, in logarithmic ratio, as they recede from the centre', offered as the cause of the concentric striation; reconstructed_in_script: E3 isotropic distance-transform front from a square nucleus: asphericity ∝ R^-0.97 (control: chessboard metric, exponent 0.00, square stays square) | E4 equal-rate Voronoi mosaic from hard-core (min separation 0.6 x mean spacing) centres, rendered with 8-px walls and measured by the same wall-segmentation pipeline as the plate: 6.10 neighbours, area CV 0.30 (Poisson centres: exact 5.78, area CV 0.56) | E5 radial Ostwald-supersaturation precipitation: 2-D b0 sweep 0.05/0.1/0.2/0.4 -> P = 1.290/1.220/1.170/1.130 (Matalon-Packter sign), 3-D a0 = 100: 7 bands; control without nucleation gap: 1 continuous deposit | E6 dielectric-breakdown sweep eta = 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2: solidity 0.79 -> 0.17 (Pearson r = -0.92), mass-radius exponent 1.92 -> 1.18 | Plates: Fig. 196 outer-radius CV 0.0036, border radial-line index +0.57, 82 striae; Fig. 195 radial-line index +0.17 around 6 landmarked nuclei vs null +0.01 ± 0.06 (z = 7.1); Fig. 197 72 cells, 6.00 neighbours, area CV 0.28, KS p = 0.64 vs same-pipeline Voronoi; Fig. 208 lamella gaps ~24 px with within-ray CV 0.21, constant-gap fit beats geometric on 9/9 rays | Analogues: Carmel ooid asphericity 0.61 (nucleus) -> 0.29 (outer), 9 lamellae; Ag2CrO4 Liesegang rings P = 1.070 (CV 0.016), geometric r = 0.9972 / RMSE 2.37 px vs arithmetic r = 0.9880 / RMSE 4.66 px, gaps increase (Spearman 0.91, p = 7e-4); transfer_boundary: 2-D plate sections and 2-D lattice models stand for 3-D spherites; the synthetic spherite texture in the GIF is a drawing driven by the 3-D E5 band radii and the Fig. 196 striae count | ooids and silver-chromate rings are modern analogues (labelled as such), not Harting's albumen spherites; does_not_claim: a quantitative match between the E5 simulation's P (1.22) and the specimen's P (1.07): chemistry and geometry differ, KS p < 0.01, reported as such | that uniform normal deposition alone explains the ooid: it rounds off faster than E3 predicts (minor-axis cortex 88 px vs major-axis 63 px) | crystallographic (calcite / vaterite / aragonite) control of fibre texture." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/morphoeval-rubric-0.1.0> ;
    pk:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/model/claude-fable-5-1> ;
    pk:assessmentType "morphoeval/0.1.0/overall" ;
    pk:evidenceAgainst "remediation needed: Add a sharp-interface diffusion solver to locate the Mullins-Sekerka critical radius directly, and a curvature-dependent accretion term to explain why the ooid rounds faster than uniform offset.",
        "remediation needed: Calibrate the E5 Liesegang solver (a0, b0, K_ss/K_sp, D) to the silver-chromate specimen so the simulated and measured spacing-ratio distributions pass a KS test, and add a CaCO3/albumen parameter set from Mercedes-Martin et al. (2016).",
        "remediation needed: Replace the two manual landmarks with an automatic detector (e.g. radial-symmetry transform) for the Fig. 195 nuclei and the Fig. 208 nucleus, and fit the ooid outer boundary with a robust ellipse that excludes touching grains." ;
    pk:hasScore 8.6e-01 ;
    pk:hasVerdict "faithful" .

